<Layouts.app flash={@flash} current_user={assigns[:current_user]}>
  <section id="hero" class="relative isolate pt-8 pb-24 sm:pb-28">
    <%!-- decorative only (exception to D4): squares crawl along the floor and
         up the walls behind the hero, driven by assets/js/landing_crawl.js --%>
    <canvas
      id="landing-crawl"
      data-crawl="hero"
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 -top-12 -z-10 block h-[calc(100%+3rem)] w-full select-none text-zinc-400 [--crawl-accent:var(--color-primary-light)] dark:text-zinc-700 dark:[--crawl-accent:var(--color-primary-dark)]"
    ></canvas>
    <div id="hero-copy" class="space-y-6">
      <.badge color="primary" variant="bordered" rounded="full" size="small">
        for Australian MSPs
      </.badge>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
        A lock outside your RMM.
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
        When an RMM is compromised, every control inside it goes too: consent prompts,
        allowlists, your own admin settings. Breakline sits at each client site, outside
        your RMM, and keeps remote access closed until a booked window or a passkey
        approval opens it.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <%= if assigns[:current_user] do %>
          <.button_link href={~p"/dashboard"} variant="default" color="primary">
            Dashboard
          </.button_link>
        <% else %>
          <.button_link
            id="hero-pilot"
            href="#pilot"
            variant="default"
            color="primary"
            icon="hero-arrow-down"
          >
            How a pilot works
          </.button_link>
          <.button_link href={~p"/sign-in"} variant="bordered" color="natural">
            Sign in
          </.button_link>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <.card
      :for={
        {icon, title, body} <- [
          {"hero-bolt", "Locks itself when your RMM is attacked",
           "Breakline checks CISA's exploited-vulnerabilities list and ACSC alerts every 30 minutes. When your tool appears, remote access closes at every client site that uses it, and only a passkey reopens it."},
          {"hero-finger-print", "Just-in-time remote access",
           "Sessions open inside a booked maintenance window or after a passkey approval, from your senior staff or from the client. Outside those times the channel stays shut."},
          {"hero-document-check", "Proof for clients and insurers",
           "Every open, close, approval and lockdown goes into a tamper-evident log. Export it as a PDF that can be checked offline."}
        ]
      }
      variant="bordered"
      color="natural"
      rounded="large"
      padding="large"
    >
      <.card_title title={title} icon={icon} size="small" font_weight="font-semibold" />
      <.card_content>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">{body}</p>
      </.card_content>
    </.card>
  </section>

  <section id="why" class="max-w-3xl space-y-4 pt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why it has to sit outside the RMM</h2>
    <p class="text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">
      In August 2026 the ACSC warned that N-able N-central was being exploited in Australia.
      Attackers used the RMM's own remote-control feature to reach client machines, then set
      up tunnels to keep their access.
    </p>
    <p class="text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">
      Anything configured inside the RMM falls with it. Breakline takes its orders only from
      policies signed in its own cloud, so someone holding your RMM console can't open it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
      What it doesn't do: it closes the door your RMM uses. It doesn't replace your endpoint
      protection, and it can't remove an attacker who is already inside a network.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="pilot" class="max-w-3xl space-y-4 pt-12 pb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">A free pilot, monitor mode first</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">
      <li>
        Install the Breakline package on a small Linux box or VM at one client site, or at
        your own office. DNS-only mode needs no rewiring.
      </li>
      <li>Enrol it from the dashboard with a one-time code.</li>
      <li>
        For a few weeks it blocks nothing. It records when your RMM and remote tools reach
        the site, so you see your own traffic before anything changes.
      </li>
      <li>Then you decide, client by client, whether to enforce.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">The first five sites are free during a pilot.</p>
    <p :if={@contact_email} id="pilot-contact" class="font-medium">
      Email <a class="underline" href={"mailto:" <> @contact_email}>{@contact_email}</a>
      to book a 20 minute walkthrough.
    </p>
    <p :if={!@contact_email} id="pilot-contact" class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
      Pilot places are by invitation while Breakline is in early testing.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layouts.app>
